= Salience =

== Overview ==
'''Salience''' is the degree to which a stimulus, feature, event, or representation stands out or receives elevated processing priority relative to alternatives.

Something can be salient because it is visually distinctive, novel, emotionally charged, motivationally important, goal-relevant, or strongly learned.

== Perceptual salience ==
A bright, moving, high-contrast, or unusual stimulus can stand out because of sensory properties.

Visual salience models often estimate local contrast across features such as color, orientation, intensity, or motion.

== Motivational salience ==
A cue can become salient because it predicts reward, threat, need, or action relevance.

This form of salience depends on learning and current state, not only physical contrast.

== Salience and attention ==
[[Attention]] is the process of selectively prioritizing information.

Salience is one factor that can influence selection.

A salient object can fail to receive attention when goals strongly favor something else, and a nonsalient object can be deliberately attended.

Therefore:

<pre>
salience ≠ attention
</pre>

== Salience and importance ==
What stands out is not necessarily what matters most.

Vivid or unusual information can dominate processing even when less conspicuous information is objectively more important.

== Salience and availability ==
The [[Availability heuristic]] uses accessibility or ease of retrieval as a cue in judgment.

Salience can influence availability, but the concepts are not identical.

